export type SermonSection = { heading: string; body: string }

export const sermonManuscripts: Record<string, SermonSection[]> = {
  "Salvation: rescued by grace and brought into Christ": [
    { heading: "The human need and the initiative of grace", body: "The gospel begins with an honest diagnosis: humanity is not merely uninformed or underdeveloped; we are sinners who have turned from the Creator. Romans 1–3 describes the universal reach of sin and removes every ground for boasting. Sin is guilt before God, corruption within human life, and a power that enslaves. If we misunderstand the depth of the problem, we will reduce salvation to advice for better living. But the good news is that God does not wait for sinners to make themselves worthy. The Father acts in love, sending the Son. At the cross, Jesus gives Himself for us; in the resurrection, God vindicates Him and inaugurates new life. Salvation is therefore God’s rescue from beginning to end. Grace is not God pretending sin does not matter; grace is God dealing with sin through Christ and welcoming the undeserving." },
    { heading: "What salvation gives and what it produces", body: "Ephesians 2:1–10 holds together our former condition, God’s mercy, salvation by grace through faith, and the good works for which we are recreated. Justification speaks of being declared right with God through Christ; reconciliation describes peace where hostility stood; adoption speaks of being welcomed as God’s children; new birth describes the beginning of life from God; sanctification concerns our growth in holiness; glorification points to the completion of salvation when God raises and renews His people. These are not competing gospels but dimensions of one saving work. Faith is not a payment or a meritorious achievement; it is the empty hand receiving Christ. Repentance is the turning of the whole person toward God. Good works matter deeply, but they are the fruit of salvation, not its purchase price. The believer’s confidence rests in Jesus, while the evidence of living faith appears in a transformed life." },
    { heading: "The call to respond and persevere", body: "The apostolic message calls people to repent and believe, to confess Jesus as Lord and to enter the life of discipleship. This response must not be manipulated by fear or reduced to repeating words without understanding. The invitation is to trust the crucified and risen Christ. Those who belong to Him are united with Him in His death and resurrection; sin is no longer to reign as master. Assurance is grounded first in God’s promise and Christ’s finished work, while the New Testament also calls believers to continue in faith, love and obedience. We do not preserve ourselves by anxious self-reliance; we depend on the faithful God who keeps His people. The hope of salvation reaches beyond present forgiveness to the resurrection of the body and the renewal of creation. Therefore preach the whole gospel: the seriousness of sin, the sufficiency of Christ, the freeness of grace, the call to faith and the new life that follows." },
  ],
  "The resurrection: the centre of Christian hope": [
    { heading: "The claim on which Christianity stands", body: "The resurrection is not an optional encouragement added to the Christian message. It is the event on which apostolic preaching stands or falls. The disciples did not announce merely that Jesus’ teaching lived on in their memories; they testified that the One who had been crucified was raised by God. The Gospel accounts present continuity and transformation: the risen Jesus is the same Jesus who suffered, yet He is no longer subject to death. Paul’s argument in 1 Corinthians 15 is uncompromising: if Christ has not been raised, preaching is empty, faith is futile and believers remain in their sins. Christianity is therefore not founded on admiration for a dead teacher but on the living Lord. The resurrection declares that the cross was not defeat, that Jesus is vindicated, and that God’s promised victory over death has begun." },
    { heading: "Firstfruits and the hope of bodily renewal", body: "Paul calls Christ the firstfruits of those who have died. The image is agricultural: the first portion of the harvest assures the arrival of the full harvest. Jesus’ resurrection is therefore both unique and representative. His people will share resurrection life when He comes. Christian hope is not escape from embodiment, as though the body were a prison from which salvation releases us. God created the body, the Son took a real human body, and the risen Christ remains bodily alive. The promise is not that our present mortal condition continues unchanged, but that God will raise and transform His people. This hope gives dignity to bodily life, compassion to the grieving, courage in suffering and meaning to ordinary labour. The final chapters of Scripture do not end with creation discarded, but with heaven and earth renewed and God dwelling with His people." },
    { heading: "Living in the power of the coming age", body: "Because Christ is risen, the future has begun to shape the present. Romans 6 teaches believers to regard themselves as alive to God in Christ and no longer to offer themselves to sin as instruments of unrighteousness. Resurrection faith changes how we endure suffering, face death, treat our bodies, conduct our work and serve the church. It does not promise that Christians will avoid grief or persecution. Jesus Himself suffered, and the New Testament gives room for lament. But grief is not hopeless, and faithful labour is not wasted. Paul concludes his great resurrection chapter by urging believers to be steadfast, immovable and always abounding in the Lord’s work. The reason is theological: in the Lord, labour is not in vain. Preach resurrection not merely as a doctrine for funerals, but as the foundation for holiness, perseverance, justice, mission and hope." },
  ],
  "God’s original design for humanity": [
    { heading: "Created by God and given dignity", body: "Genesis opens with God, not humanity. The world exists because the Creator speaks, orders and blesses. Human beings appear as creatures within this good world, yet they receive a distinctive vocation: to bear God’s image and exercise delegated rule. This means human worth is received, not achieved. It cannot be measured by wealth, education, ethnicity, physical ability, age or social usefulness. Male and female are both included in the image-bearing commission; neither is presented as an inferior class of human. Psalm 8 marvels that God gives humanity honour and responsibility while reminding us that this dignity is bestowed. The doctrine should reshape how we regard children, the elderly, the poor, strangers and those whose lives are often treated as disposable. To honour the Creator is to take seriously the dignity of those He has made." },
    { heading: "Vocation, work and relationships in God’s world", body: "Genesis 1 and 2 describe humanity’s calling through fruitfulness, cultivation, keeping and responsible dominion. Rule is not permission to exploit. It is delegated authority exercised under God, for the flourishing of what belongs to Him. The garden account joins work with worshipful responsibility: humanity is placed in the garden to serve and keep it. Work is therefore not simply a curse or a means of personal status; meaningful labour belongs to the original good order, although toil and frustration enter after the fall. Human beings are also created for relationship—with God and with one another. The man’s recognition of the woman as corresponding partner resists the idea that human life is designed for isolated self-sufficiency. Family, community, cooperation and mutual responsibility are part of creaturely life. The Sabbath pattern reminds humanity that we are not machines; we live by receiving God’s provision and honouring His rhythm." },
    { heading: "Christ restores the human vocation", body: "The New Testament identifies Jesus as the image of the invisible God and the true pattern of renewed humanity. Where Adam’s story is marked by disobedience, Christ’s obedience brings life. Where human authority has become domination, Jesus demonstrates rule through service and self-giving love. The church is called to put on the new humanity, being renewed in knowledge according to the image of its Creator. This does not mean Christians become divine by nature or that human beings cease to be creatures. It means that, by grace, our distorted vocation is being restored in union with Christ. We learn to represent God through truth, holiness, mercy, stewardship and reconciliation. The doctrine of creation should therefore lead to worship and responsibility. Ask not only, “What rights do I possess?” but also, “How can I faithfully reflect the Creator in the world He has entrusted to us?”" },
  ],
  "The fall of humanity": [
    { heading: "The temptation to distrust God", body: "Genesis 3 presents sin as a rupture that begins with distorted speech about God. The serpent questions the divine command and suggests that God is withholding what would make humanity wise. The temptation is not simply to eat forbidden fruit; it is to distrust the goodness of the Creator and seize moral autonomy. The humans take what is not given, and the consequences appear immediately: shame, hiding, fear and blame. Sin promises freedom but produces bondage. It promises wisdom but fractures fellowship. The narrative exposes a recurring pattern: temptation misrepresents God, invites us to define good on our own terms, and then leaves us defending ourselves rather than walking openly with Him. The passage calls us to examine the stories we believe about God, especially when obedience feels costly." },
    { heading: "The spread of sin and death", body: "Genesis 4 shows that the rupture in Eden does not remain private. Jealousy grows into violence; brother turns against brother. The rest of Scripture traces sin through personal desire, family life, social structures and nations. Romans 5 explains the solidarity of humanity in Adam and contrasts Adam’s trespass with Christ’s righteous act. Paul is not excusing individual responsibility; he is explaining why sin and death have such universal dominion. Romans 8 adds that creation itself groans under corruption. The fall therefore has spiritual, moral, relational and creational dimensions. We should avoid reducing it to a single isolated mistake or treating suffering as proof that a particular sufferer has committed a particular sin. The Bible gives a wider account: all humanity lives in a world disordered by rebellion, and all need God’s rescue." },
    { heading: "Judgment, mercy and the promise of restoration", body: "God confronts the humans with questions, names the consequences of their actions and sends them from the garden. Judgment is real; the way to the tree of life is guarded. Yet the story is not abandoned to despair. God clothes the humans, preserves life and gives a promise that the serpent’s hostility will not have the final word. Christians read the unfolding story in the light of Jesus, who enters the human condition, resists temptation, bears sin and defeats death. The language of Adam and Christ in Romans 5 and 1 Corinthians 15 helps us see that redemption is not merely a return to innocence but the beginning of a new humanity under the faithful Son. The proper response to the fall is neither denial nor fatalism. Confess sin honestly, refuse to blame others, seek reconciliation where possible and place hope in the Christ who makes all things new." },
  ],
  "Redemption: delivered for God": [
    { heading: "Redemption as rescue and belonging", body: "The Bible’s redemption language is grounded in concrete acts of rescue. Israel remembers the Exodus as the Lord bringing His people out of slavery with a mighty hand. The family redeemer in Israel’s life acts to protect the vulnerable and preserve family inheritance. These backgrounds help us hear the New Testament’s claim that Christ redeems. The cross is not an abstract religious symbol; it is God’s decisive action to liberate people from sin and bring them into covenant belonging. Jesus gives His life as a ransom, and His blood is associated with the new covenant. Redemption means that believers no longer belong to the powers from which God has rescued them. They are His people, called to worship and serve Him." },
    { heading: "The cost and accomplishment of Christ’s work", body: "The New Testament uses several images to explain the cross: sacrifice, ransom, reconciliation, victory, covenant and justification. No single image exhausts the mystery. Together they show that God deals with guilt, hostility, bondage and evil through the self-giving Son. The cross reveals both the seriousness of sin and the depth of divine love. It is important not to imagine the Father and Son as divided in purpose: the New Testament presents the Son willingly giving Himself in obedience and the Father acting in love to save. The resurrection announces that death has not held Him. Redemption is accomplished by Christ, not supplemented by human merit. Our service, generosity and holiness are grateful responses to a rescue we could never achieve for ourselves." },
    { heading: "Freedom for a new life", body: "Biblical freedom is not simply the absence of restraint. Israel was brought out of Pharaoh’s service to worship the Lord; Christians are delivered from sin so that they may live as servants of righteousness and love. Titus 2 connects redemption with purification and zeal for good works. This guards against two errors: legalism, which treats obedience as the price of acceptance, and license, which treats grace as permission to continue in destructive patterns. The redeemed are called to reject exploitation, practise mercy and stand with those who suffer bondage and injustice. The language of belonging should produce humility rather than superiority. We remember that we were rescued by grace. Let the sermon move hearers from gratitude to consecration: because Christ has given Himself for us, we offer our lives to Him." },
  ],
  "The image of God": [
    { heading: "The image is bestowed, not earned", body: "Genesis does not say that only powerful or impressive people bear God’s image. The statement concerns humanity as humanity, male and female. The image is a gift from the Creator and a calling to represent Him within creation. Scholars have described this in terms of representation, relationship, moral capacity and vocation; the text especially links image-bearing with humanity’s delegated rule. These dimensions need not be treated as mutually exclusive, but conclusions should be grounded in the passage. The central pastoral consequence is clear: no human being is disposable. Christian discipleship must oppose contempt, abuse, racism, exploitation and every practice that denies the dignity God has bestowed." },
    { heading: "The image after the fall and in Christ", body: "Genesis 9:6 grounds the seriousness of murder in the fact that humanity is made in God’s image. James 3:9 similarly rebukes the use of the tongue to curse people made in God’s likeness. These passages show that sin has not erased human dignity. Yet Scripture also describes humanity as distorted and in need of renewal. The New Testament presents Christ as the perfect image of God. He makes the Father known and displays faithful human obedience. Believers are being transformed into His image by the Spirit and renewed according to the Creator’s likeness. Christian growth is therefore not self-invention; it is restoration into the life of Christ." },
    { heading: "The ethical force of image-bearing", body: "If people bear God’s image, our theology must affect how we speak and act. We cannot worship God while degrading those He has made. We cannot celebrate spiritual gifts while tolerating cruelty, or defend human dignity only when it benefits our own group. Image-bearing calls for protection of life, truthful speech, just treatment, patient instruction and practical care. It also gives a foundation for mission: every neighbour is someone to whom the good news can be offered with respect. At the same time, the image doctrine does not mean every human desire is morally good; dignity and moral accountability belong together. The church honours people while calling all people to reconciliation with God through Christ." },
  ],
  "Sacred space: Eden to new creation": [
    { heading: "Eden and the pattern of divine presence", body: "The Bible begins with God placing humanity in a garden where His presence, provision and human vocation belong together. Genesis 2 describes rivers, trees and the task of serving and keeping the garden. Later biblical writers use sanctuary and priestly language that invites comparison with Eden, though the details should be argued carefully rather than assumed. The larger pattern is that human beings are made to live before God, receive His word and serve within His world. Exile from Eden is therefore more than a change of location: it represents broken fellowship and restricted access to life. The rest of Scripture tells the story of God making a way to dwell with His people." },
    { heading: "Tabernacle, temple and the presence among Israel", body: "The tabernacle is built so that God may dwell among His people. Its design, priesthood, sacrifices and boundaries teach that the Holy One is graciously present but must not be approached casually. The temple continues this role in Israel’s life, becoming a place of worship and covenant memory. Yet the prophets warn that sacred buildings cannot protect a people who practise injustice and refuse repentance. God desires covenant faithfulness, not ritual used to conceal disobedience. Ezekiel’s vision of restoration holds out hope for renewed presence after judgment. These passages teach reverence and grace together: God chooses to dwell among His people, and His presence calls them to holiness, justice and worship." },
    { heading: "Jesus, the church and the new creation", body: "John announces that the Word became flesh and tabernacled among us. Jesus speaks of His body as the temple, identifying Himself as the meeting place between God and humanity. Through His death and resurrection, access to God is opened. Paul describes the church as a temple of the Holy Spirit and a dwelling place of God by the Spirit. This is a corporate identity: believers are joined together, not merely isolated individuals who each claim private sacred status. Revelation brings the theme to its climax. In the new creation, God dwells with humanity; the city needs no separate temple because God and the Lamb are its temple. Sacred space is thus not a technique for controlling God. It is the story of God graciously restoring communion with His people through Christ." },
  ],
  "The supernatural worldview of Scripture": [
    { heading: "The Bible’s world is larger than what eyes can see", body: "A biblical worldview begins with the conviction that the visible creation is not the whole of reality. Scripture speaks of angels, heavenly worship, spiritual opposition, visions and divine action. These accounts are not invitations to invent elaborate systems wherever the text is quiet. Nor should they be dismissed simply because they describe realities beyond ordinary observation. The first safeguard is the Creator-creature distinction: the Lord alone is eternal, uncreated and sovereign. Everything else, visible or invisible, owes its existence to Him. The unseen realm is not a second kingdom equal to God. It is part of the created order under His authority." },
    { heading: "Spiritual conflict and the supremacy of Christ", body: "The New Testament acknowledges spiritual opposition. Ephesians 6 describes a struggle that cannot be reduced to human conflict alone, yet its armour is strikingly ethical and gospel-shaped: truth, righteousness, readiness from the gospel of peace, faith, salvation, God’s word and prayer. Colossians declares that Christ is before all things and that hostile powers are disarmed through the cross. The church therefore does not fight through fear, obsession or attempts to gain secret knowledge. It stands in what God has provided. Jesus’ authority is not fragile, and believers are not instructed to magnify evil beings. They are instructed to remain faithful, resist the devil, pray and hold fast to Christ." },
    { heading: "Discernment without fear or speculation", body: "A mature supernatural worldview is both open to Scripture and disciplined by Scripture. Read narratives according to their literary setting; distinguish what the text states from what interpreters infer. Do not build doctrines from sensational testimony, folklore or isolated phrases. Do not attribute every illness, conflict or disappointment to a demon. The Bible recognises spiritual evil, but it also recognises ordinary human responsibility, bodily weakness and the complexity of life in a fallen world. The proper fruit of this teaching is worship, courage, holiness and compassion. The unseen realm should make us more dependent on God, not more fascinated with darkness. Keep the eyes of faith fixed on Jesus, the risen Lord over all things." },
  ],
  "The unseen realms: heavenly council, angels and powers": [
    { heading: "Heavenly court imagery in the Old Testament", body: "Several Old Testament passages portray the Lord in the midst of a heavenly assembly or court. Job opens with heavenly beings presenting themselves before God; 1 Kings 22 depicts a heavenly council scene; Psalm 82 describes God standing in an assembly and judging among the elohim; Daniel 7 presents a throne room and heavenly court. These texts use imagery familiar to their ancient setting to communicate the Lord’s kingship and the accountability of heavenly agents. The passages should be read individually. A shared image does not mean every scene has identical participants or purpose. The Lord is never one deity among equals: He presides, judges and rules." },
    { heading: "Difficult texts, textual questions and responsible interpretation", body: "Some passages require particular care. Deuteronomy 32:8 has a well-known textual difference among ancient witnesses, with readings commonly rendered “sons of Israel” or “sons of God”/“divine beings.” A responsible teacher should acknowledge the textual evidence and explain why translations differ rather than hiding the issue. The meaning of elohim also depends on context; it can refer to the God of Israel and, in certain contexts, other spiritual beings. A word study alone cannot settle the identity of every figure. Psalm 82 has been interpreted in more than one way, including readings that emphasise unjust human judges and readings that understand a heavenly assembly. Present the arguments fairly and distinguish firm conclusions from debated reconstructions." },
    { heading: "The New Testament focus: Christ above every power", body: "Whatever conclusions one reaches about particular heavenly-council passages, the New Testament’s central emphasis is unmistakable: all things were created through and for Christ, and every rule and authority is subordinate to Him. Hebrews presents the Son as superior to angels; Colossians warns against allowing spiritual intermediaries to displace Christ; Ephesians locates the church’s struggle within a larger spiritual conflict while directing believers to stand in God’s strength. This study should enlarge reverence for God, not produce fascination with spiritual hierarchies. The church’s confidence is not based on mastering the unseen realm but on belonging to the One who made and rules it. Worship God alone, test claims carefully and live faithfully." },
  ],
  "The gifts of the Spirit": [
    { heading: "The Spirit gives gifts for service", body: "Paul’s teaching on spiritual gifts begins with the Spirit’s work and the lordship of Jesus. In 1 Corinthians 12, diversity is not a problem to eliminate; it is part of God’s design for one body. The same Spirit distributes different manifestations, and each is given for the common good. Romans 12 and Ephesians 4 add other lists and emphases, reminding us that no single list should be treated as an exhaustive catalogue. Gifts are not trophies that establish a spiritual ranking. They are gracious provisions for service. The church should therefore honour quiet faithfulness as well as visible ministry and should make room for members to contribute according to the grace God gives." },
    { heading: "Love, order and discernment in the exercise of gifts", body: "First Corinthians 13 stands between Paul’s teaching on gifts because love is indispensable to their proper use. A person may speak impressively, possess knowledge or display remarkable sacrifice, yet without love the activity is empty. Chapter 14 then insists that gathered worship should build up the church. Speech that cannot be understood requires interpretation if it is to serve the congregation; contributions should be orderly and weighed. This does not quench the Spirit. It recognises that the Spirit who gives gifts is not the author of confusion. Churches should test teaching, maintain accountability and refuse manipulation. No claimed gift exempts a person from biblical character or correction." },
    { heading: "Discovering and stewarding what God gives", body: "The practical question is not, “How can I appear gifted?” but, “How can I faithfully serve the people God has placed around me?” Gifts may be developed through practice, wise mentoring, study and opportunities to serve. Character must grow alongside competence. Leaders should avoid using gift language to create celebrity culture or excuse misconduct. Members should not despise their contribution because it seems less visible than another person’s. Peter calls believers to serve one another as stewards of God’s varied grace. The church is healthiest when every member serves under Christ, gifts are exercised in love, and all glory returns to God." },
  ],
  "The fruit of the Spirit": [
    { heading: "The conflict between flesh and Spirit", body: "Galatians 5 is written to churches tempted to misunderstand Christian freedom. Paul rejects both legalism and self-indulgence. Freedom is not an opportunity for the flesh; through love believers serve one another. The works of the flesh describe patterns that damage worship, relationships and community. Against them Paul sets the fruit of the Spirit. The contrast is not between naturally pleasant people and difficult people; it is between life governed by the old order and life shaped by God’s Spirit. The Spirit does not merely add religious excitement to an unchanged character. He forms a new way of living." },
    { heading: "One fruit with many expressions", body: "Paul uses the singular “fruit” and then names qualities such as love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness and self-control. These qualities belong together as a harvest of Spirit-formed character. Love cannot be separated from truth; gentleness is not cowardice; patience is not approval of injustice; self-control is not merely outward restraint. Jesus provides the pattern: His love is holy, His courage is gentle, His obedience is steadfast and His joy is rooted in the Father. The fruit is not a substitute for spiritual gifts, but it is essential evidence of mature Christian life. Public ministry without Christlike character contradicts the message it claims to serve." },
    { heading: "Keeping in step with the Spirit", body: "Paul concludes by calling believers to keep in step with the Spirit and refusing conceit, provocation and envy. This is both divine work and active discipleship. Believers depend on the Spirit, yet they are commanded to put away destructive conduct and practise what is good. Growth is often visible in ordinary moments: the restrained reply, the patient conversation, the honest confession, the forgiveness offered, the generous act no one applauds. Churches should teach the fruit as a way of life rather than a decorative list to memorise. Ask the Spirit to expose the places where the flesh still governs, then take concrete steps of obedience in the strength God supplies." },
  ],
  "Spiritual growth: maturity into Christ": [
    { heading: "Maturity is the goal of discipleship", body: "The New Testament does not define maturity as age, religious vocabulary, public platform or the number of meetings attended. Ephesians 4 describes the goal as growing into the fullness of Christ, no longer being easily deceived and learning to speak the truth in love. Hebrews 5 rebukes hearers who have remained untrained in discernment. Spiritual maturity includes knowledge, but knowledge must become wisdom, endurance and love. It is possible to know many doctrines and still behave selfishly. It is also possible to be active in ministry while neglecting the hidden life with God. Growth is measured by increasing conformity to Jesus." },
    { heading: "The means through which God forms us", body: "Scripture, prayer, fellowship, worship, service, correction and suffering all appear in the New Testament as contexts in which believers grow. The word of God renews the mind and equips the church for every good work. Prayer deepens dependence. Fellowship gives encouragement and accountability. Service teaches humility. Trials can produce endurance when received in faith, though suffering should never be romanticised or imposed on vulnerable people. The Spirit is the source of transformation, and believers respond by putting off the old patterns and putting on the new humanity. No single spiritual practice replaces the others; together they form a life attentive to God." },
    { heading: "A lifelong process sustained by grace", body: "Growth is often gradual. There are seasons of visible progress and seasons when obedience feels costly. The Christian should neither become complacent nor fall into despair. Philippians 1:6 grounds confidence in God’s continuing work; Philippians 2:12–13 calls believers to work out what God is working within them. This is not a contradiction but the pattern of grace-enabled obedience. Churches should cultivate patient discipleship, not comparison and performance. Set realistic habits of Scripture, prayer, fellowship and service. Seek help when patterns of sin persist. Celebrate growth without boasting, and remember that the goal is not to become impressive but to become more like Christ." },
  ],
  "Devotion to prayer": [
    { heading: "Prayer begins with relationship and revelation", body: "Biblical prayer is not an attempt to persuade a reluctant deity to become kind. Jesus teaches His disciples to address God as Father, and the Psalms show believers bringing praise, grief, confession, questions and requests before Him. Prayer rests on who God has revealed Himself to be. The Lord is holy and sovereign, yet He invites His people to draw near. The Lord’s Prayer gives a pattern: God’s name, kingdom and will come first; daily provision, forgiveness and deliverance are then brought before Him. This order trains our desires as well as our words." },
    { heading: "Prayer shaped by the person and work of Christ", body: "The New Testament presents Jesus as the one mediator and the great high priest through whom believers approach God with confidence. This confidence is not arrogance; it is access secured by Christ. Prayer in Jesus’ name is not a verbal formula attached to any wish. It means praying in relation to His person, authority and purposes. The Spirit helps believers in weakness, and Paul’s prayers often ask for inner strengthening, knowledge of God’s will, love, discernment and fruitful lives. These prayers broaden our expectations beyond immediate circumstances. We should ask for daily needs, but also for spiritual wisdom and perseverance." },
    { heading: "Building a faithful life of prayer", body: "Devotion to prayer grows through a sustainable rhythm rather than occasional intensity alone. Set aside regular time, pray Scripture, make room for thanksgiving and confession, bring specific requests, and learn to wait. The Psalms teach us to lament honestly without abandoning trust. Jesus warns against empty repetition and display, while also commending persistence. Prayer should lead to obedience: we cannot ask for God’s kingdom while refusing His will in our relationships and responsibilities. Nor should unanswered prayer be used to shame sufferers or claim that every outcome is controlled by the intensity of someone’s faith. Pray boldly, trust humbly and remain faithful." },
  ],
  "Devotion to the Gospel": [
    { heading: "The gospel is news about Jesus", body: "The gospel is not first a programme for self-improvement; it is the announcement of what God has done in Jesus Christ. Paul summarises the apostolic message in 1 Corinthians 15: Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures, was buried, and was raised on the third day. The Gospels show the kingdom announced and embodied in Jesus’ ministry. The cross deals with sin; the resurrection declares His victory and lordship. The gospel includes the call to repent and believe, but the response does not replace the news itself. We announce a finished work and invite people to receive the life God gives." },
    { heading: "The gospel creates a new people", body: "The gospel reconciles people to God and brings them into a community that crosses former boundaries. Ephesians 2 describes Christ making peace and creating one new humanity, breaking down hostility. Therefore the gospel cannot be reduced to private forgiveness while ignoring reconciliation, holiness and the church. Those who receive grace become ambassadors of reconciliation. The message also confronts every rival allegiance: wealth, status, ethnicity, political power and self-rule cannot take the place of Jesus as Lord. Gospel faithfulness includes compassion and justice, but it must not be diluted into generic moral improvement. Its centre remains the crucified and risen Christ." },
    { heading: "Guarding and sharing the good news", body: "Paul urges Timothy to guard the good deposit and entrust it to faithful people who can teach others. The church must therefore preserve the gospel’s content while communicating it clearly in each setting. Avoid adding human achievement as a condition of acceptance, and avoid removing the call to repentance and new life. Do not use the gospel as a tool of manipulation, financial exploitation or personal celebrity. Teach it to children, new believers and mature Christians; the church never outgrows its need for grace. A gospel-shaped life should make the message visible through truth, humility, generosity and courage. Pray for open doors, speak with clarity and trust God for the response." },
  ],
  "Praying for people: intercession shaped by Scripture": [
    { heading: "Intercession is participation in love", body: "Intercession means bringing other people and their needs before God. It is not a way to control them from a distance or to display spiritual superiority. Biblical intercessors pray for rulers, communities, enemies, the sick, the church and those who have not yet heard the gospel. Their concern reflects God’s compassion and purposes. Abraham’s prayer for Sodom, Moses’ pleading for Israel and Paul’s prayers for the churches show that intercession can be bold, thoughtful and deeply relational. The intercessor stands before God with concern for others, while remembering that God alone is sovereign and knows every heart." },
    { heading: "Let Scripture teach us what to ask", body: "Paul’s prayers in Ephesians 1 and 3, Philippians 1 and Colossians 1 are especially rich patterns for intercession. He asks that believers know God more deeply, receive wisdom, be strengthened inwardly, grow in love and bear fruit. These prayers do not ignore practical needs; they place them within the larger purpose of spiritual maturity. We can pray for healing, provision, protection and open doors while also asking for endurance, discernment, reconciliation and holiness. Scripture helps prevent our prayers from becoming only a list of immediate preferences. It enlarges our vision to include God’s glory and the good of His people." },
    { heading: "Praying faithfully without taking God’s place", body: "Intercession should lead to practical love. If we pray for the hungry, we should be ready to help where we can; if we pray for reconciliation, we must pursue truthful and safe steps toward peace. We should not promise outcomes God has not promised, blame sufferers when a request is not answered as hoped, or treat prayer as a technique that guarantees control. Keep praying, listen, seek wise counsel and act responsibly. Pray by name, record requests, follow up with care and give thanks honestly when answers come. The church becomes a praying community when people carry one another before God and alongside one another in daily life." },
  ],
  "Adventures in prayer: the breadth of biblical prayer": [
    { heading: "The Bible gives many forms of prayer", body: "Prayer in Scripture is not one repeated formula. The Psalms include praise, lament, confession, thanksgiving, protest, trust and requests for help. Hannah prays from deep distress; Solomon asks for wisdom; Daniel confesses communal sin; Jesus gives thanks, intercedes and submits to the Father in Gethsemane. These examples teach us that prayer can be emotionally honest and theologically reverent. We do not need to pretend that grief is joy or that confusion has disappeared. The Psalms give language for bringing the whole human experience before God." },
    { heading: "Bold asking, persistence and surrender", body: "Jesus teaches persistence through parables and invites His disciples to ask, seek and knock. Yet He also teaches submission to the Father’s will. Gethsemane is essential: Jesus expresses the anguish of the coming suffering and entrusts Himself to the Father. Biblical boldness is therefore not demanding that God fulfil our preferred script. It is confidence in His goodness joined to surrender to His wisdom. The apostles pray for boldness in witness, healing, provision and endurance. Their prayers are communal and mission-shaped, not merely centred on private comfort." },
    { heading: "Learning the breadth of prayer in daily life", body: "A rich prayer life makes room for different biblical patterns. Begin with adoration: name who God is. Confess honestly. Give thanks specifically. Bring requests for yourself and others. Lament where life is painful. Pray Scripture when words are difficult. Wait quietly without treating impressions as equal to the written word. Join with other believers and let prayer lead to action. Over time, revisit requests and notice both answers and seasons of waiting. The adventure of prayer is not chasing extraordinary experiences; it is learning to live in continual dependence on the Father through Christ by the Spirit." },
  ],
  "Evangelism: bearing witness to Jesus": [
    { heading: "The content of the witness", body: "Evangelism is the announcement of good news, not a contest in which Christians must win arguments. The message is that Jesus is the promised Messiah and Lord, crucified for sins and raised by God. Through Him forgiveness and new life are offered. The apostles adapted their explanations to their hearers, but they did not replace the centre of the message. In Acts 2 Peter speaks from Israel’s Scriptures; in Acts 17 Paul begins with the worldview of his Athenian audience. In both settings he leads people toward the true God and the risen Jesus. Faithful evangelism knows the message well enough to explain it simply." },
    { heading: "The manner of witness", body: "The manner of evangelism must agree with the message. Peter calls believers to give a reason for their hope with gentleness and respect. Paul asks for gracious speech, wisdom and open doors. This excludes coercion, deception, humiliation and pressure tactics. Listen to people’s questions; understand their background; speak truthfully about what you know and admit what you do not know. Compassion is not compromise, and boldness is not harshness. Jesus welcomed people, confronted hypocrisy and spoke truth with authority. His followers should imitate His integrity and care." },
    { heading: "From conversation to discipleship", body: "Evangelism does not end when someone expresses interest or makes a first response. Jesus commands the making of disciples, teaching them to obey all He commanded. New believers need Scripture, prayer, fellowship, pastoral care and a community in which faith can grow. Follow up without treating people as numbers or projects. Respect their pace while continuing to invite them toward Christ. The church should also examine whether its shared life makes the gospel credible: love, holiness, generosity and reconciliation should adorn the message. Pray for people by name, seek natural opportunities and trust the Spirit to work through faithful witness." },
  ],
  "Psalm 23: the Lord our shepherd": [
    { heading: "The Lord is the shepherd who provides", body: "Psalm 23 begins with a personal confession: the Lord is my shepherd. The image draws on the ancient responsibility of a shepherd to guide, feed and protect the flock, and on Israel’s wider confession that God shepherds His people. Green pastures and still waters portray provision and restoration, not a guarantee of luxury or an uninterrupted life. The psalm says the Lord provides what is needed for the journey under His care. The statement “I shall not want” is therefore an expression of trust in the Shepherd, not a formula promising every desire." },
    { heading: "The valley and the presence of God", body: "The poem moves from peaceful provision to a dark valley. The change is important: the Shepherd’s care is not proved by the absence of danger. The psalmist walks through the valley because the Lord is with him. The rod and staff evoke protection and guidance. The language becomes more intimate: “thou art with me.” Scripture does not teach that faith removes grief, illness, persecution or death from human experience. It teaches that God’s presence sustains His people within it. This is why Psalm 23 can comfort the grieving without offering shallow explanations for suffering." },
    { heading: "The table, the goodness and the lasting welcome", body: "The scene changes again: the Lord prepares a table in the presence of enemies, anoints the head with oil and causes the cup to overflow. The image is one of welcome and honour even while opposition remains. The psalm ends with goodness and mercy pursuing the worshipper and the hope of dwelling in the Lord’s house. Jesus identifies Himself as the good shepherd who lays down His life for the sheep. Read Psalm 23 through that revelation: our confidence rests in the Shepherd who knows His own and gives Himself for them. Follow His voice, trust His presence and learn to shepherd others with care." },
  ],
  "How to study your Bible": [
    { heading: "Begin with observation", body: "Faithful Bible study begins by listening. Before asking what a passage means to us, ask what is actually there. Read the paragraph repeatedly. Notice who is speaking, who is addressed, what happens, what commands or promises appear, and what words are repeated or contrasted. Read the surrounding chapter and, where possible, the whole book. A verse detached from its argument can be made to say almost anything. Observation slows us down and challenges our tendency to see only what we expected to find. Pray for humility and attentiveness, but do not substitute an impression for careful reading." },
    { heading: "Interpret in context and in the whole canon", body: "Interpretation asks what the author communicated to the original audience through the passage’s words, structure and genre. Narrative, poetry, prophecy, wisdom, Gospel, letter and apocalyptic writing use different forms and should not be read as though they were identical. Historical background can illuminate a text, but it should serve the text rather than dominate it. Compare translations and cross-references, checking each reference in context. Then consider the passage’s place in the unfolding biblical story and its relation to Christ. Avoid claiming that every resemblance is a direct prediction or that every command applies in precisely the same covenantal form." },
    { heading: "Move from meaning to faithful response", body: "Application should grow from interpretation. Ask what the passage reveals about God, humanity, sin, grace, worship and the life of God’s people. Consider whether it gives a command to obey, a promise to trust, an example to follow or avoid, a warning to heed, or a truth to believe. Apply it personally and communally. A passage about generosity may challenge both private habits and church structures; a passage about forgiveness may require repentance, wise boundaries and a pursuit of peace. Use reliable study resources, but test conclusions against Scripture. The goal is not to collect interesting facts; it is to become a doer of the word." },
  ],
  "Why biblical theology matters": [
    { heading: "The Bible tells one unfolding story", body: "Biblical theology studies the Bible’s themes as they develop through its books and historical movements. It begins by respecting each passage in its own setting, then asks how it contributes to the larger story. Creation, fall, covenant, exodus, kingdom, exile, promise, Messiah, Spirit, church and new creation are not disconnected subjects. They form an unfolding account of God’s purposes. This approach protects us from treating the Bible as a collection of unrelated moral sayings. It also protects us from flattening every passage into a generic statement about Jesus without first hearing what the passage meant in its original context." },
    { heading: "Trace themes without forcing connections", body: "Consider the theme of divine presence: God dwells with humanity in Eden, with Israel in the tabernacle and temple, among us in the incarnate Son, within the church by the Spirit, and finally with humanity in the new creation. The theme develops, and each stage contributes something distinctive. Similarly, the Exodus becomes a pattern of deliverance, but not every journey is automatically an Exodus typology. The New Testament itself identifies some patterns and fulfilments; where it does not, interpreters should speak with appropriate care. Good biblical theology recognises both continuity and development, promise and fulfilment, shadow and reality, without pretending that every detail has the same function." },
    { heading: "Christ at the centre of the canon", body: "Jesus teaches His disciples to read the Scriptures in relation to His suffering and glory. This does not mean the Old Testament loses its original meaning; rather, the New Testament shows how God’s promises reach their goal in Christ. The law, prophets and writings bear witness to the purposes of God that come to fulfilment through Him. Biblical theology therefore deepens worship and strengthens discernment. It helps the church resist proof-texting, appreciate the diversity of Scripture and proclaim the gospel from the whole counsel of God. Study the parts carefully, trace the story patiently and let the completed revelation in Christ shape Christian understanding." },
  ],
  "The believer’s love walk: walking in the love of Christ": [
    { heading: "Love begins in God, not in our performance", body: "The Christian command to love rests on the prior love of God. First John does not tell believers to manufacture divine love through willpower; it says that love is from God and that God has shown His love by sending His Son. The cross is the measure and source of Christian love. We love because He first loved us. This means the believer’s love walk begins with receiving grace, abiding in Christ and allowing the Spirit to shape the heart. Love is not a way to earn acceptance, but neither can a claim to know God remain indifferent to the needs of others. The one who has received mercy is called to become merciful." },
    { heading: "The shape of Christlike love", body: "Jesus gives His disciples a new command: love one another as He has loved them. His love is not sentimental approval; it is humble, truthful and sacrificial. John 13 places the command beside the washing of feet and the approaching cross. First Corinthians 13 exposes loveless religion: impressive speech, knowledge, faith or sacrifice cannot replace patient, kind and enduring love. Romans 12 describes love without hypocrisy, honouring others, sharing with those in need and refusing revenge. The love walk is therefore visible in ordinary conduct—how we speak when irritated, how we treat those who cannot repay us, whether we forgive, whether we tell the truth and whether we seek another person’s good." },
    { heading: "Love, truth, boundaries and the enemy", body: "Biblical love does not call evil good or require a person to remain in danger. It rejoices with the truth, protects the vulnerable and seeks justice. Forgiveness does not erase the need for accountability, repentance or wise boundaries. Jesus commands love for enemies, including prayer for those who persecute us; this rejects revenge and dehumanisation, but it does not make abuse acceptable. The church must not use the language of submission or forgiveness to silence victims. Instead, it should embody truthful compassion, protect those at risk and pursue appropriate justice. The love walk reaches beyond friends and familiar circles to neighbours, strangers and opponents." },
    { heading: "The Spirit-formed practice of love", body: "Galatians 5 connects freedom with serving one another through love. The fruit of the Spirit includes love, patience, kindness, gentleness and self-control; these qualities must shape the use of spiritual gifts, leadership and ministry. A gifted person who humiliates others contradicts the purpose of the gift. A church that speaks of revival while tolerating bitterness and partiality needs to return to Christ’s command. Practise love deliberately: listen carefully, speak truth without cruelty, forgive where repentance is present, give generously, honour people and pray for those who oppose you. Ask the Lord to make the character of Jesus visible in your relationships. The proof of discipleship is not a slogan but a life increasingly governed by His love." },
  ],
  "A life of worship and thanksgiving": [
    { heading: "Worship is a response to God’s worth", body: "In Scripture, worship begins with God’s self-revelation. Israel is called to worship because the Lord is Creator, Redeemer and covenant King. The Psalms summon the whole person to praise: memory, voice, body, attention and trust. The New Testament directs worship through Jesus Christ, who reveals the Father and opens the way into His presence. Music is a powerful gift within gathered worship, but worship is larger than singing. The biblical language includes bowing, serving, offering, thanksgiving and obedience. We worship because God is worthy, not merely because a song moves us or a meeting creates a particular atmosphere." },
    { heading: "The danger of worship without obedience", body: "The prophets repeatedly confront worship that is separated from justice and covenant faithfulness. Sacrifices and songs cannot be used to hide oppression, dishonesty or indifference to the poor. Jesus likewise warns against honouring God with lips while the heart remains far away. This does not make gathered worship unimportant; it means gathered worship must form a faithful people. Romans 12 calls believers to present their bodies as living sacrifices. Everyday choices—integrity at work, care for family, generosity, sexual holiness, forgiveness and service—belong within the life of worship. The church should not measure worship only by musical excellence or emotional intensity." },
    { heading: "Thanksgiving in every season", body: "Thanksgiving is the discipline of remembering God’s goodness and confessing dependence. It does not require denying pain or pretending that every circumstance is good. The Psalms show lament and thanksgiving existing within the same life of faith. Believers can grieve honestly while trusting God’s character. Hebrews calls the church to offer praise and to do good and share with others, joining words with action. Let gratitude become specific: remember provision, forgiveness, the faithfulness of God, the gift of community and the hope of resurrection. Worship should send us into the world as people who reflect the mercy and holiness of the One we praise." },
  ],
  "The kingdom of God and the mission of the church": [
    { heading: "The kingdom announced by Jesus", body: "The kingdom of God is central to Jesus’ preaching. It refers to God’s reign and saving rule, not merely a territory or a human institution. The prophets anticipated a time when God would judge evil, restore His people and establish righteous rule. Jesus announces that the kingdom has drawn near and demonstrates its arrival through teaching, healing, deliverance and the gathering of disciples. His parables reveal both the kingdom’s surprising growth and its demand for a response. The kingdom is not simply a synonym for heaven after death; it concerns God’s action in history and His purpose to renew all things." },
    { heading: "Already present, not yet complete", body: "The New Testament presents the kingdom as inaugurated in Jesus’ ministry, death and resurrection, while still awaiting its final consummation. Believers experience forgiveness, the Spirit and a new allegiance now, yet they continue to face suffering, injustice and death. This is why Jesus teaches His disciples to pray, “Thy kingdom come.” The church must resist triumphalism, which claims that present circumstances already display the fullness of God’s reign, and despair, which acts as though Christ has not begun His work. Christian hope lives between promise fulfilled and promise awaited." },
    { heading: "The church’s mission under the King", body: "The church does not own the kingdom or replace the King. It bears witness to Jesus through gospel proclamation, disciple-making, holiness, mercy, justice, reconciliation and service. The mission is not a licence to dominate others or baptise partisan power. Jesus defines authority through service and sends His people to make disciples among all nations. The church’s public life should offer a credible sign of the King’s character while its words announce the crucified and risen Lord. We work faithfully without imagining that human effort will complete the kingdom. Our hope rests in Christ’s return and the renewal of creation." },
  ],
}
